  "worked_solution": "Approaching from the left, we have $x<2,$ so we use the expression $g(x)={x}^{2}+x-2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}({x}^{2}+x-2) \\\\  & ={2}^{2}+2-2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x\\ge 2,$ so we use the expression $g(x)=\\frac{3-\\sqrt{x+7}}{{x}^{2}-1}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}\\frac{3-\\sqrt{x+7}}{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-\\sqrt{2+7}}{{2}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-\\sqrt{9}}{4-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-3}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{3} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}g(x)=4,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
